{"id":"workloads-pods-user-namespaces","corpus":"k8s","title":"User Namespaces","sourceUrl":"https://kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/workloads/pods/user-namespaces/","license":"Kubernetes Documentation © The Kubernetes Authors, licensed CC BY 4.0","licenseUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/","blocks":[{"kind":"h1","text":"User Namespaces"},{"kind":"p","text":"More information about this feature"},{"kind":"p","text":"This is a stable feature in Kubernetes, and has been since version v1.36. It was first available in the v1.28 release. You can no longer disable or opt out of this feature or behavior (it is locked); if you explicitly set a value for the associated feature gate UserNamespacesSupport, Kubernetes ignores it but does not report any error."},{"kind":"p","text":"This page explains how user namespaces are used in Kubernetes pods. A user namespace isolates the user running inside the container from the one in the host."},{"kind":"p","text":"A process running as root in a container can run as a different (non-root) user in the host; in other words, the process has full privileges for operations inside the user namespace, but is unprivileged for operations outside the namespace."},{"kind":"p","text":"You can use this feature to reduce the damage a compromised container can do to the host or other pods in the same node. There are several security vulnerabilities rated either HIGH or CRITICAL that were not exploitable when user namespaces is active. It is expected user namespace will mitigate some future vulnerabilities too."},{"kind":"h2","text":"Before you begin"},{"kind":"p","text":"This is a Linux-only feature and support is needed in Linux for idmap mounts on the filesystems used. This means:"},{"kind":"li","text":"On the node, the filesystem you use for /var/lib/kubelet/pods/, or the custom directory you configure for this, needs idmap mount support."},{"kind":"li","text":"All the filesystems used in the pod's volumes must support idmap mounts."},{"kind":"p","text":"In practice this means you need at least Linux 6.3, as tmpfs started supporting idmap mounts in that version. This is usually needed as several Kubernetes features use tmpfs (the service account token that is mounted by default uses a tmpfs, Secrets use a tmpfs, etc.)"},{"kind":"p","text":"Some popular filesystems that support idmap mounts in Linux 6.3 are: btrfs, ext4, xfs, fat, tmpfs, overlayfs."},{"kind":"p","text":"In addition, the container runtime and its underlying OCI runtime must support user namespaces. The following OCI runtimes offer support:"},{"kind":"li","text":"crun version 1.9 or greater (it's recommend version 1.13+)."},{"kind":"li","text":"runc version 1.2 or greater"},{"kind":"p","text":"To use user namespaces with Kubernetes, you also need to use a CRI container runtime to use this feature with Kubernetes pods:"},{"kind":"li","text":"containerd: version 2.0 (and later) supports user namespaces for containers."},{"kind":"li","text":"CRI-O: version 1.25 (and later) supports user namespaces for containers."},{"kind":"p","text":"You can see the status of user namespaces support in cri-dockerd tracked in an issue on GitHub."},{"kind":"h2","text":"Introduction"},{"kind":"p","text":"User namespaces is a Linux feature that allows you to map users in the container to different users in the host. Furthermore, the capabilities granted to a pod in a user namespace are valid only in the namespace and void outside of it."},{"kind":"p","text":"A pod can opt-in to use user namespaces by setting the pod.spec.hostUsers field to false."},{"kind":"p","text":"The kubelet will pick host UIDs/GIDs a pod is mapped to, and will do so in a way to guarantee that no two pods on the same node use the same mapping."},{"kind":"p","text":"The runAsUser, runAsGroup, fsGroup, etc. fields in the pod.spec always refer to the user inside the container. These users will be used for volume mounts (specified in pod.spec.volumes) and therefore the host UID/GID will not have any effect on writes/reads from volumes the pod can mount. In other words, the inodes created/read in volumes mounted by the pod will be the same as if the pod wasn't using user namespaces."},{"kind":"p","text":"This way, a pod can easily enable and disable user namespaces (without affecting its volume's file ownerships) and can also share volumes with pods without user namespaces by just setting the appropriate users inside the container (RunAsUser, RunAsGroup, fsGroup, etc.). This applies to any volume the pod can mount, including hostPath (if the pod is allowed to mount hostPath volumes)."},{"kind":"p","text":"By default, the valid UIDs/GIDs when this feature is enabled is the range 0-65535. This applies to files and processes (runAsUser, runAsGroup, etc.)."},{"kind":"p","text":"Files using a UID/GID outside this range will be seen as belonging to the overflow ID, usually 65534 (configured in /proc/sys/kernel/overflowuid and /proc/sys/kernel/overflowgid). However, it is not possible to modify those files, even by running as the 65534 user/group."}],"lemmas":{"namespaces":["namespaces","namespace"],"featuring":["featuring","feature"],"featured":["featured","feature"],"stables":["stable","stables"],"stabled":["stabled","stable"],"kubernetes":["kubernetes","kubernete"],"versions":["version","versions"],"v1s":["v1s","v1"],"firsts":["first","firsts"],"availables":["availables","available"],"releases":["release","releases"],"releasing":["releasing","release"],"released":["released","release"],"nos":["nos","no"],"longer":["longer","long"],"longers":["longer","longers"],"longs":["longs","long"],"longed":["longed","long"],"longing":["longing","long"],"disables":["disables","disable"],"disabled":["disabled","disable"],"disabling":["disabling","disable"],"opts":["opts","opt"],"opted":["opted","opt"],"opting":["opting","opt"],"behaviors":["behavior","behaviors"],"locked":["locked","lock"],"lockeds":["lockeds","locked"],"locks":["lock","locks"],"locking":["locking","l